frontends/nss: stop conntrack window-checking a connection it no longer sees

Conntrack stops seeing a connection's packets the moment the firmware starts
forwarding them. Its TCP window state therefore freezes at the last packet
that went through the host, and is advanced again only by the stats sync,
once a second (ECM_NSS_IPV4_STATS_SYNC_PERIOD).

Any packet that returns to the host path inside that window is judged against
a window that can be thousands of bytes behind: a slow-path exception, the
first packet after a deceleration, or simply a short flow that completes
between two syncs. tcp_in_window() fails it, conntrack marks it INVALID, and
OpenWrt's stock egress rule for a masqueraded zone

	oifname "wan" ct state invalid ... drop

drops it. Nothing records the loss, because from the stack's point of view the
firewall did its job.

Marking the connection liberal is what the kernel's own offload paths do for
the same reason - nft_flow_offload.c, act_ct.c and xt_FLOWOFFLOAD.c all set
IP_CT_TCP_FLAG_BE_LIBERAL on both directions when they take a flow away from
conntrack, and nf_conntrack_proto_tcp.c reads it before the pernet sysctls.
Doing it per connection keeps window validation for every flow the firmware is
not carrying, which the global nf_conntrack_tcp_no_window_check switch does
not.

The flag has to be hidden from the NO_SEQ_CHECK test above it. That test asks
whether conntrack's window numbers are worth enforcing, and answers yes unless
something already marked the connection liberal - a mid-stream pickup, where
tcp_new() sets the flag because conntrack has no window state to give. Our own
flag must not answer that question: ECM hands the firmware real window numbers
from the last sync, and the firmware sees every accelerated packet, so it stays
the one party able to check them. Without the guard the first acceleration is
fine and every re-acceleration - the same nf_conn re-entering this path after a
decel - would read our flag back and silently switch the firmware's sequence
checking off for the rest of the connection's life.

The flag is deliberately not cleared on deceleration: the packets that follow
one are exactly the packets this protects.

IPv4 and IPv6 are line-for-line identical here and change together.

